<p>Disruption tied to Iran and the Strait of Hormuz has hit oil and gas flows, jolting energy markets and forcing governments and businesses across Asia and the Gulf to respond. The coverage shows a region dealing with immediate supply uncertainty while the rest of the world recalibrates around one of the most important maritime energy routes. In the Gulf, financial reserves and infrastructure buffers are helping absorb some of the conflict’s immediate damage. Across Asia, governments are moving to conserve energy and protect consumers as lower oil and gas flows increase pressure on import-dependent economies.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/9eoLy0a3fNboV_DZbHUnaRgCYC8CKOMOP3UkruMsQ_0Q8qCAG3G5yXiUolmGXJQFdfG7PrTLml88FtytneJx_2sXvfd5fpmpedc81cwiLlW7_9EUWTKlhtBCGr9W-uZCxmPlLY-q4ov81CJZRL79nYwIgrus-El3OhuED2wAkvEw75Sdh0wW0qwVvUXvNBx6JS8aSwr1QtML8SzJurd33cmJ3mvNOK88qV_uVl0dwJBpNuoeGZ7o-jCMn4uWMks7z8w3QN2um8vzV2o' alt='Energy-security image published with coverage of disruption around the Strait of Hormuz' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Asset-risk assumption: Analysts’ relatively manageable Gulf scenario depends on most regional energy assets staying largely intact even as the conflict continues.</li><li>Bypass capacity: Saudi Aramco’s export pipeline that bypasses the Strait of Hormuz is one of the main tools analysts cite to keep some crude exports moving during maritime disruption.</li><li>Country recalibration: India, the United States, and Russia are rethinking energy strategy as shifting oil flows and rising dependence reshape energy geopolitics.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Gulf analysts: Their base case is that the heaviest military phase could last two to four more weeks. In that timeframe, sovereign wealth funds, reserves, and infrastructure buffers could prevent systemic stress on currencies and banks. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/19/2026/in-the-iran-war-the-gulfs-shock-absorber-is-still-oil'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Asian governments: They are focusing on demand restraint and consumer protection as supply disruption shows how quickly conflict near Hormuz can pressure fuel-importing economies. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/asia-scrambles-conserve-energy-iran-war-disrupts-oil-131217018'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>The Hindu analysis: It presents the closure of the Strait of Hormuz as a geopolitical turning point, one that is reshaping oil flows and increasing the strategic importance of alternative suppliers and routes. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/oil-power-and-politics-of-disruption/article70761839.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/19/2026/in-the-iran-war-the-gulfs-shock-absorber-is-still-oil'>In the Iran war, the Gulf’s shock absorber is still oil</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/oil-power-and-politics-of-disruption/article70761839.ece'>Oil, power, and politics of disruption</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/asia-scrambles-conserve-energy-iran-war-disrupts-oil-131217018'>Asia scrambles to conserve energy as Iran war disrupts oil and gas supplies</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/UpliftingNews/comments/1ry8aht/iran_war_energy_shock_sparks_global_push_to/'>Iran war energy shock sparks global push to reduce fossil fuel dependence</a> - reddit.com</li></ul>